WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.060
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.370
I want to talk today about what's going on
in the Central African Republic, but first

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:04.700
a couple prefatory notes.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.350
One, in the US especially, we tend to image
Africa monolithically as, like, poor and undeveloped

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:13.930
and generally other, even though it is a large
continent full of vastly different nations.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:16.581
And, while today we are going to talk about
an African state that has struggled more or

00:00:16.581 --> 00:00:20.919
less continually since its independence, it's
worth remembering that seven of the world's

00:00:20.919 --> 00:00:24.029
ten fastest growing economies are in Africa.

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:26.960
Two, this is speculation, but I think one
of the reasons we don't hear about what’s

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.410
going on in the Central African Republic,
is that it's complicated.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:34.480
You know, we like narratives, like the Rebels
versus the Empire, or Harry versus Voldemort,

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.040
or Dora versus Swiper the fox.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.570
I'm sorry; I watch a lot of children's TV.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:42.540
Like, we can stand some shades of grey in
our heroes and our villains, but we love to

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.480
know who the heroes are and who the villains
are.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.690
And I think a lot of times when new stories
don't fit that narrative, we just ignore them

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:53.510
because the idea of good guys and bad guys
is so central to our understanding of ourselves

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.180
and our world that we can't abandon it.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:00.040
And I also think, if I can tangent on a tangent,
that this is part of why our interest lags

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.140
when international news stories grow, like,
less narratively powerful.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.710
Like, it's easy to root for the rebels in
Cairo's Tahrir Square when they want freedom

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.649
and democracy, but the actual complex business
of rebuilding a state, as we’ve seen in

00:01:11.649 --> 00:01:14.329
Egypt and elsewhere, gets somewhat less attention.

00:01:14.329 --> 00:01:18.369
Okay, with all that noted, the Central African
Republic is here in, you guessed it, the centre

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:21.799
of Africa; although, to be fair, it has not
often been much of a republic.

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:26.159
Life expectancy in the CAR is 51 and it's
one of the poorest countries in the world

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:27.159
today.

00:01:27.159 --> 00:01:30.290
So after declaring independence from France
in 1960, there were several decades of military

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:35.600
rule, but then in 1993, there were multi-party
elections, and the elected president was Ange-Félix

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.930
Patassé, and initially, the economy grew
under Patassé, but he failed to be able to

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:44.229
pay the military and civil servants which
is, like, one of the central things that governments

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:45.229
do.

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:47.149
Also there was quite a bit of corruption,
and there were several coup attempts over

00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:48.149
the next decade.

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:53.280
So Patassé remained president until 2003
when a military coup finally succeeded.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.880
Military leader François Bozizé succeeded
by using the time-honored tactic of waiting

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.920
until the president was out of town and then
quickly seizing the government.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.660
So obviously the CAR has long struggled with
security and political stability, like in

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:08.090
the east of the country, the Lord's Resistance
Army, led by Joseph Kony whom you might remember

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.549
from his brief moment of 'Kony 2012' internet
fame.

00:02:11.549 --> 00:02:12.549
Yeah, that guy.

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:14.709
His group has been murdering and torturing
people for decades.

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:18.480
In fact, it's believed by many that Kony is
hiding out in the Central African Republic.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.901
But also, both the Patassé and Bozizé regimes
relied on foreign armies from Chad and the

00:02:22.901 --> 00:02:27.130
Democratic Republic of Congo and elsewhere
to help, like, shore up their governments.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:32.290
But, security-wise, after Bozizé took power
in 2003, things got significantly worse.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:36.090
So there was this group, the Union of Democratic
Forces for Unity that combined with other

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:40.541
rebel groups and began to fight against Bozizé's
regime in what came to be known as the Central

00:02:40.541 --> 00:02:41.959
African Bush War.

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:45.850
This involved lots of groups, like the People's
Army for the Restoration of Democracy, and

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:49.730
the Convention of Patriots for Justice and
Peace, but anyway, they all banded together

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.400
and tried to take down the government.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.400
It didn't work.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.550
Finally there was a peace treaty in 2007,
but the fighting never, like, totally stopped.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.810
Okay, so flash forward to 2012: several of
the groups from the Central African Bush War

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.000
unite with some other rebel groups to form
Séléka which means 'union.'

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.209
And most members of Séléka belong to the
CAR's Muslim minority which is about 15% of

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:10.430
the country's population.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:13.439
They quickly take control of a bunch of towns
in the north and the centre, and then other

00:03:13.439 --> 00:03:18.390
African countries, including Chad, send in
troops to try to protect the capital of Bangui.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:22.480
But it doesn't work, so in March 2013, the
capital of the Central African Republic, Bangui,

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.500
falls to the Séléka rebels, and Bozizé
flees the country.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:31.020
And one of Séléka's leaders, a Muslim, Michel
Djotodia, becomes president, but almost as

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.480
soon as Séléka takes power, there's a lot
of in-fighting among all of these groups that

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.730
formed the union; like, it's easy to unify
when you're all battling the government, but

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:42.210
then when you have the power, everybody kinda
wants some of the power.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:46.069
Meanwhile, there was never anything approaching
peace, and Séléka militias committed all

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:47.069
kinds of atrocities.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:50.830
Like, according to Human Rights Watch, they
burned dozens of towns and villages, they

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:55.569
shot fleeing civilians in the back, and also
a huge percentage of the population has been

00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:56.569
displaced.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:59.969
There are tens of thousands of Christians
who’ve fled to the Bangui airport and have

00:03:59.969 --> 00:04:01.060
remained there for months.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:06.050
And now we come to the Christian anti-balaka,
or anti-sword militias.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:11.159
These anti-balaka groups have sought to respond
to Séléka’s violence with much more violence,

00:04:11.159 --> 00:04:15.400
and the anti-balaka mass murder of members
of the Muslim minority is now being called

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.459
by many a genocide or at least the beginning
of one.

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:22.880
So in January 2014, President Djotodia resigned
and was replaced by a woman named Catherine

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.350
Samba-Panza, but there isn’t currently much
of a government to lead in the Central African

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:28.350
Republic.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.840
Last week, the UN approved and 11,000 member
security force that will hopefully bring an

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.850
end to the violence, but for now it continues.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.980
Hank, like I said earlier, this conflict is
difficult and complicated.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.850
It looks nothing like, you know, Hobbits versus
Sauron.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:41.880
But that isn’t only the case in the Central
African Republic.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.410
I mean who were the good guys in the American
Revolution?

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.310
The Americans were for freedom and democracy,
right?

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:50.250
Well yeah, except for slaves who probably
would have been much better off under the

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.250
rule of England.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:55.850
Or what about the Vietnam War or the French
Revolution or World War One in which so many

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:57.430
people died for so little?

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:01.210
Hank, we can’t just ignore stories that
don’t fit our understanding of the world

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:05.060
and we also shouldn’t try to, like, change
them to make them fit our preconceived narratives

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.060
of humanity.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.280
We have to make room in our stories for the
world as we find it.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.270
And the Central African Republic reminds us
that war is not, finally, a story of good

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:16.150
versus evil as much as we might wish it were
so.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:19.760
Let’s hope the UN peacekeepers arrive soon
and can live up to their name.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:20.680
I’ll see you on Friday.

